    "lawCode": "Renum §459-g to be §459-h, add §459-g, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5036\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the social services law, in relation\nto prohibiting the condition of benefits and services received by\nvictims of domestic abuse on the cooperation of the perpetrator of\nsuch abuse\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL : To ensure the confidentiality and\nsafety of victims of domestic violence.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS : The bill would amend the Social\nServices Law by adding a new Section 459-h. The section provides that\nneither the state nor any political subdivision thereof, public\nauthority, or employee thereof shall compel a victim of domestic\nviolence to contact his or her abuser. The section further provides\nthat in the event such contact must be made, the governmental entity\nshall provide an intermediary to make such contact on behalf of the\nvictim in a manner that will protect the privacy confidentiality and\ncurrent location of the victim.\n\n JUSTIFICATION : Women seeking social services from New York State\nmust undergo a stringent screening process that requires them to\nprovide numerous forms of documentation. While requiring a great deal\nof documentation before providing services is an admirable goal for\nthe state, this policy has the unintended consequence of putting many\nbattered women at risk and even denying some of them access to\nservices such as public housing. This is because a victim of domestic\nviolence or abuse is often forced to contact her abuser in order to\nobtain all of the documentation required by the state. When women are\nunable to do this or refuse to contact their abuser, they are denied\nstate services.\n\nWomen across the state, from Westchester to Buffalo, have been denied\nor threatened with denial of social services unless they make contact\nwith their abuser. This scenario does not only affect women living in\nlarge cities such as New York City; cases of women being forced to\ncontact their abusers have also been documented in Livingston County\nand other upstate counties.\n\nWomen who have had orders of protection against men who are extremely\nviolent have been put in jeopardy when they produced these orders of\nprotection at the department of social services and were told such\norders \"didn't matter\" and they would still have to contact their\nabusers to get the proper documentation, or they would not qualify for\nfood stamps or any other benefits. The screening process for most\nforms of public assistance requires that the applicant produce three\npieces of identification (such as a utility bill or credit card\nstatement) stating that a person convicted of a crime (such as\ndomestic violence) does not live with the applicant at the present\ntime. This is putting many domestic violence survivors at risk,\ncausing them to make contact with their former abusers, whom they are\ntrying so desperately to escape.\n\nIn the case of the domestic violence victim, confidentiality and\nseparation is essential for survival. Requiring men or women to make\ncontact with their former abusers is endangering their well-being.\nThis bill will ensure that any agency or authority cannot compel a\ndomestic violence victim to contact his or her abuser.\n\nRecognizing that at times contact must be made to comply with state or\nfederal mandates, the bill requires such contact be through a\nconfidential intermediary to be provided by the governmental entity\nthat requires such information in a way that protects the victim.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :  2007-08: S2962 Referred to Social\nServices, Children and Families 2005-06: S6608 Referred to Social\nServices, Children and Families 2003-04: S6128 Referred to Children\nand Families, Committed to Rules\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS : To be determined.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE : This act shall take effect on the ninetieth day\nafter it shall have become a law.\n",
